{"task_id": "Mbpp/160", "solution": "def _extended_gcd_positive(a, b):\n    # Computes gcd(a, b) and coefficients x, y such that ax + by = gcd(a, b).\n    # Assumes a, b are non-negative.\n    if a == 0:\n        return b, 0, 1\n    gcd_val, x1, y1 = _extended_gcd_positive(b % a, a)\n    x = y1 - (b // a) * x1\n    y = x1\n    return gcd_val, x, y\n\ndef find_solution(a, b, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds integer solutions (x, y) for the linear Diophantine equation ax + by = n.\n    Returns (x, y) as a tuple, or None if no solution exists.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Handle special cases where a or b (or both) are zero.\n    if a == 0 and b == 0:\n        if n == 0:\n            return (0, 0)\n        else:\n            return None\n    elif a == 0:  # b != 0\n        if n % b == 0:\n            return (0, n // b)\n        else:\n            return None\n    elif b == 0:  # a != 0\n        if n % a == 0:\n            return (n // a, 0)\n        else:\n            return None\n\n    # General case: a != 0 and b != 0.\n    # Calculate gcd(abs(a), abs(b)) and corresponding Bezout coefficients x_g_abs, y_g_abs\n    # such that abs(a)*x_g_abs + abs(b)*y_g_abs = g.\n    g, x_g_abs, y_g_abs = _extended_gcd_positive(abs(a), abs(b))\n\n    # If n is not divisible by g, no integer solution exists.\n    if n % g != 0:\n        return None\n\n    # Calculate the scaling factor to match n from the Bezout's identity.\n    scale_factor = n // g\n\n    # Adjust x_g_abs and y_g_abs based on the original signs of a and b.\n    # _extended_gcd_positive assumes positive inputs for a and b.\n    # If original 'a' was negative, the 'x' coefficient should be flipped.\n    # Similarly for 'b'.\n    x_particular = x_g_abs\n    y_particular = y_g_abs\n\n    if a < 0:\n        x_particular = -x_particular\n    if b < 0:\n        y_particular = -y_particular\n\n    # Scale the particular solution (x_particular, y_particular) to ax + by = n.\n    x_base = x_particular * scale_factor\n    y_base = y_particular * scale_factor\n\n    # Adjust the solution to find a specific representation (e.g., smallest non-negative x).\n    # The general solution for x is x = x_base + k * (b / g).\n    # We want x in the range [0, abs(b/g)).\n    mod_val_x = abs(b // g)\n    \n    # Calculate one possible x value in the desired range.\n    x = x_base % mod_val_x\n    if x < 0:\n        x += mod_val_x\n\n    # Calculate the corresponding y value for this chosen x.\n    # From ax + by = n, we have by = n - ax, so y = (n - ax) / b.\n    y = (n - a * x) // b\n\n    return (x, y)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/166", "solution": "import itertools\n\ndef find_even_pair(numbers: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of pairs of integers in a list that XOR to an even number.\n    Handles empty and single-element lists by returning 0.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        return 0\n\n    even_xor_count = 0\n    # Generate all unique pairs from the input list using itertools.combinations\n    for a, b in itertools.combinations(numbers, 2):\n        # Calculate the XOR sum of the pair\n        xor_result = a ^ b\n        # Check if the XOR result is an even number\n        if xor_result % 2 == 0:\n            even_xor_count += 1\n\n    return even_xor_count"}
