{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(num: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Set the leftmost unset bit in the binary representation of num.\"\"\"\n\n    def _set_bit_at_position(n: int, position: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Set the bit at the specified position to 1.\"\"\"\n        return n | (1 << position)\n\n    def _find_left_most_unset_bit(n: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Find the position of the leftmost unset bit (highest index).\"\"\"\n        if n == 0:\n            return 0\n\n        target_pos = -1\n        # Iterate from the highest significant bit down to 0.\n        # This identifies the leftmost unset bit within the number's current bit length.\n        for pos in range(n.bit_length() - 1, -1, -1):\n            if (n >> pos) & 1 == 0:\n                target_pos = pos\n                break\n\n        if target_pos != -1:\n            return target_pos\n        else:\n            # If all bits up to n.bit_length() - 1 are set (e.g., 0b111),\n            # the leftmost unset bit is at the next position.\n            return n.bit_length()\n\n    # Find the position of the leftmost unset bit.\n    pos_to_set = _find_left_most_unset_bit(num)\n    \n    # Set the bit at the identified position.\n    return _set_bit_at_position(num, pos_to_set)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/389", "solution": "def find_lucas(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the n'th Lucas number using an iterative approach.\n    Handles base cases L(0)=2 and L(1)=1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 2\n    elif n == 1:\n        return 1\n    elif n < 0:\n        # As per instructions, do not raise exceptions for edge cases.\n        # Lucas numbers are typically defined for non-negative integers.\n        # Returning an undefined value (e.g., None) or 0 could be an option,\n        # but for this context, assuming valid n >= 0 based on problem scope.\n        # If negative n is explicitly required, its definition would be needed.\n        # For competitive programming style, often assumes valid input unless specified.\n        # Given n must be an index, negative n doesn't have a standard definition here.\n        return 0 # Or could be 2 if L(-n) = L(n), but that's not standard\n    else:\n        a, b = 2, 1  # L(0) and L(1)\n        for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n            next_lucas = a + b\n            a = b\n            b = next_lucas\n        return b"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/388", "solution": "def highest_Power_of_2(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the highest power of 2 that is less than or equal to n.\n    Assumes n is a positive integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    # The bit_length() method returns the number of bits required to represent n in binary.\n    # The highest power of 2 less than or equal to n is 2 raised to the power of (bit_length - 1).\n    return 1 << (n.bit_length() - 1)"}
